  "result": {
    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.maximum_flow - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.maximum_flow.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximum_flow()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute maximum flow from source to sink.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximum_flow()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Compute maximum flow from source to sink.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.maximum_flow()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Compute maximum flow from source to sink.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCompute maximum flow from source to sink.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L75337-L75520){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.maximum_flow(\n    source,\n    target,\n    capacity=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Maximum flow](../assets/graphs/maximum-flow.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A directed network from source S to sink T through A and B. Each edge is labeled flow over capacity, and every edge carries flow, drawn in the accent color with width growing with the flow. The card lists the flow per edge, which totals 5.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nFinds the maximum amount of flow that can be sent from the source node to the sink node\nthrough the network. Uses the Edmonds-Karp algorithm (Ford-Fulkerson with BFS), which\nguarantees `O(VE²)` time complexity.\n\nEach edge has a capacity, the most flow it can carry. With the default `capacity=None`,\nan edge's capacity is taken from its `capacity` attribute if it has one, otherwise from\nits weight on a weighted graph, and otherwise it is `1.0`. You can also name a custom\ncapacity attribute. Parallel edges between the same two nodes add to the total capacity\nbetween them, but each keeps its own capacity and reports its own flow. Self-loops carry\nno flow.\n\nMaximum flow models how much of something can move through a network with limited link\ncapacities, for example:\n\n- throughput of a pipeline, power grid, or transport network\n- bandwidth available between two hosts in a communication network\n- assignment and matching problems that reduce to flow (e.g. bipartite matching)\n\nOn a directed graph flow only moves along edge direction. On an undirected graph each\nedge can carry flow either way, and the value reported for it is the net flow. Several\ndifferent flow assignments can achieve the same maximum; this method returns one of\nthem. The method does not modify the graph.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node the flow starts from, given as an integer node ID or a string label.\n\n<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node the flow ends at, given as an integer node ID or a string label. Must be a\n    different node than `source=`.\n\n<code>[capacity]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the edge attribute to use as capacity. Every edge must hold a numeric value\n    for it (`\"weight\"` also accepts the edge weight of a weighted graph); otherwise a\n    `ValueError` is raised. If `None` (default), each edge's `capacity` attribute is used\n    when present, then its weight on weighted graphs, and `1.0` otherwise.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Dictionary mapping `edge_id -> flow amount` for edges with positive flow. Edges that\n    carry no flow are omitted, so the dictionary is empty when the sink is unreachable.\n    Use `maximum_flow_value()` if you only need the total flow value.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If source or sink doesn't exist, they're the same node, any capacity is negative, or\n    an edge lacks the attribute named by `capacity=`.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~maximum_flow_value`) \n\n:   The total flow alone.\n\n[](`~minimum_cut`) \n\n:   The partition of the nodes that limits the flow.\n\n[](`~minimum_edge_cut`) \n\n:   The edges that limit the flow.\n\n[](`~minimum_cost_flow`) \n\n:   The cheapest flow of a given amount.\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nThe Edmonds-Karp variant of Ford-Fulkerson is used: at each step it augments along\nthe *shortest* augmenting path (measured in edge count) found by BFS, which bounds\nthe total number of augmentations by $O(V \\cdot E)$ and gives an $O(V \\cdot E^2)$\noverall runtime regardless of the actual capacities. Undirected edges are internally\ntreated as antiparallel pairs with equal capacity, and self-loops carry no flow. The\nmax-flow min-cut theorem guarantees that the total flow equals the capacity of the\nminimum $s$-$t$ cut; with unit capacities that is the edge count reported by\n`minimum_cut_value()`. Use `maximum_flow_value()` when only the number is needed.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nOn a weighted graph the weights are the capacities. At most 15 units can get from S to T,\nsince that's all the two edges out of S can carry. The result maps each edge ID to the flow\non it, and storing it as an edge attribute lines it up with the capacities:\n\n::: {#babd1292 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"S\", \"A\", 10), (\"S\", \"B\", 5), (\"A\", \"B\", 15), (\"A\", \"T\", 10), (\"B\", \"T\", 10)],\n    directed=True,\n)\n\nflow = graph.maximum_flow(\"S\", \"T\")\n\ngraph.set_edge_attrs(\"flow\", flow).get_edge_df()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<div><style>\n.dataframe > thead > tr,\n.dataframe > tbody > tr {\n  text-align: right;\n  white-space: pre-wrap;\n}\n</style>\n<small>shape: (5, 6)</small><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\"><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>source</th><th>target</th><th>rel</th><th>flow</th><th>weight</th></tr><tr><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>i64</td><td>null</td><td>f64</td><td>f64</td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td>null</td><td>10.0</td><td>10.0</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>3</td><td>null</td><td>5.0</td><td>5.0</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2</td><td>3</td><td>null</td><td>null</td><td>15.0</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>2</td><td>4</td><td>null</td><td>10.0</td><td>10.0</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>3</td><td>4</td><td>null</td><td>5.0</td><td>10.0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nNodes are numbered in the order they first appear, so S is 1 and T is 4. Edge 3, from A\nto B, carries nothing (and so has no entry in the result), because A can send all it gets\nstraight to T.\n\n### How much water reaches the city\n\nThe pipeline network is a city's water supply, with each pipe's `capacity` in megaliters a\nday, which `maximum_flow()` reads without being told. Storing the flow on every pipe, and a\n\"flow/capacity\" label, shows how the most water gets from the reservoir to the city:\n\n::: {#913429c3 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline = ec.pipeline()\n\nflow = pipeline.maximum_flow(\"Reservoir\", \"City\")\ncapacity = pipeline.get_edge_attrs(\"capacity\")\npipeline.set_edge_attrs(\"flow\", {e: flow.get(e, 0) for e in capacity})\npipeline.set_edge_attrs(\n    \"load\", {e: f\"{flow.get(e, 0):g}/{c}\" for e, c in capacity.items()}\n)\n\npipeline.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", edge_width_by=\"flow\",\n    edge_label_by=\"load\", edge_opacity=0.7, width=700, height=360,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div class=\"ec-plot\" style=\"position:relative;width:fit-content;max-width:100%;margin:1em auto\">\n<style>.ec-plot > svg { display:block; max-width:100%; height:auto; }</style>\n<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"700\" height=\"360\" viewBox=\"0.0 14.0 684.4 352.0\" style=\"font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;\">\n<rect x=\"0.0\" y=\"14.0\" width=\"684.4\" height=\"352.0\" fill=\"var(--ect-3239c383)\"/>\n<g opacity=\"0.7\"><line x1=\"435.7\" y1=\"76.0\" x2=\"435.7\" y2=\"207.6\" stroke=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\" stroke-width=\"1.17\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"435.7,215.4 433.3,207.6 438.2,207.6\" fill=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\"/></g>\n<line x1=\"435.7\" y1=\"76.0\" x2=\"435.7\" y2=\"215.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"transparent\" stroke-width=\"8.0\" pointer-events=\"stroke\" data-edge=\"7-8-0\" data-attrs='{\"id\": 10, \"edge\": \"Valley Junction \\u2192 Ridge Tank\", \"capacity\": 10, \"cost\": 1, \"flow\": 0, \"load\": \"0/10\"}'/>\n<g opacity=\"0.7\"><line x1=\"451.7\" y1=\"60.0\" x2=\"471.6\" y2=\"60.0\" stroke=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\" stroke-width=\"5.07\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"505.4,60.0 471.6,70.6 471.6,49.4\" fill=\"var(--ect-e5dfd507)\"/></g>\n<line x1=\"451.7\" y1=\"60.0\" x2=\"505.4\" y2=\"60.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"transparent\" stroke-width=\"9.1\" pointer-events=\"stroke\" data-edge=\"7-9-1\" data-attrs='{\"id\": 11, \"edge\": \"Valley Junction \\u2192 North District\", \"capacity\": 30, \"cost\": 1, \"flow\": 25.0, \"load\": \"25/30\"}'/>\n<g opacity=\"0.7\"><line x1=\"194.6\" y1=\"231.4\" x2=\"315.8\" y2=\"231.4\" st